	•	Section 2 Comments/Quotes: The final study population comprised “64,964 patients,” with mean age “51.4 ± 11.9 years” and “22% (N = 13,607) of the transplanted patients were female.” Table 2 reports extensive donor and recipient clinical-history variables, including diagnosis, vascular disease, stroke after listing, dialysis, ventilation, ECMO, prior transplantation, prior cardiac surgery, bilirubin, creatinine, PRA, donor hypertension, smoking, cocaine history, and cause of death. Race/ethnicity was not reported in the retrieved excerpts.  ￼

Section 3: Intervention & AI Methods
	•	Primary ML Component: Yes
	•	Study Design: Retrospective Cohort
	•	AI Model Architecture: ANN/MLP/NN
	•	Algorithm Name: Fully connected multilayer perceptron artificial neural network (ANN/MLP)
	•	Input Variables (Data Modality): Tabular (EMR); Donor metrics
	•	Comparator / Standard of Care: Linear Risk Score
	•	Validation Method: Cross-Validation
	•	Explainability / Interpretability Used: No
	•	Feature Selection Method: Automated
	•	Hyperparameter Tuning Reported: Yes
	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: The authors developed “a non-linear artificial neural networks (ANN) model” using fully connected MLP architectures with up to three hidden layers, and compared it with the RADIAL model. The model used donor-recipient tabular variables available at transplant. Validation used an internal “nested K-fold cross-validation approach” with an outer and inner stratified 5-fold loop. Model selection used random search over hyperparameters. Variable selection was automated via permutation testing and model-based ranking. No SHAP/LIME-style interpretability was reported.  ￼

Section 4: AI Quality & Reproducibility (CONVINCE Standards)
	•	Missing Data Handling: Multiple Imputation
	•	Class Imbalance Addressed: Yes
	•	Code Availability: Other
	•	Data Preprocessing / Normalization Described: Yes
	•	Training Size (N): NR
	•	Test Size (N): NR
	•	Section 4 Comments/Quotes: Missingness was substantial, and the authors explicitly evaluated “multiple probability imputation (MPI)” as well as mean imputation; MPI during training with mean imputation during validation is best aligned with multiple imputation among the restricted choices. Class imbalance was addressed with a weighted error function, where positive samples were weighted threefold. Preprocessing included normalization of continuous variables to zero mean/unit variance, binary coding, and one-hot encoding for categorical variables. Fixed train/test counts were not reported because validation used nested 5-fold cross-validation across the whole cohort. The software stack was reported (Theano, Lasagne, scikit-learn), but I did not see a public code-release statement, so code availability is marked Other rather than No.  ￼

